PSEUDOTERMINAL (PTY) / VIRTUAL 80x24 SCREEN

/dev/ptmx          Master, exclusive O_RDWR
/dev/pts/0         Slave, after opening master
/dev/pts/0.screen  80x24 ANSI text; inverse and underlined cursor markers
/dev/pts/0.key     Named-key write (UP, DOWN, LEFT, RIGHT, ENTER,
                   ESC or ESCAPE, TAB, SPACE or SP, BACKSPACE or BS,
                   DELETE, CTRL_A ... CTRL_Z)

A user application can attach standard FDs 0..2 to the slave, then
spawn a child. Master read/write still transfers raw PTY bytes.
The kernel additionally interprets common ANSI cursor/erase/reverse
commands for .screen. CSI 6n returns current cursor position.
This is not a complete terminal emulator. No scrollback or resizes.

MCP: use terminal_open({command:'fd'}), terminal_read({}),
terminal_send({key:'DOWN'}), terminal_read({}), terminal_close({}).
terminal_send only reports accepted input bytes; it does not wait
for rendering or return a screen. terminal_read always returns the
current screen; it may be read while a program is still drawing.
Ordinary commands should use execute_command to reduce traffic.

The mcpd process and its launching shell are protected from kill
by MCP descendants. This is not authentication or full isolation.

The standard pty_test command can relay: pty_test fd, pty_test sh,
pty_test vi /tmp/test.txt. Ctrl+C interrupts the foreground task.

MCP terminal_read forwards .screen without separate cursor coordinates;
cursor-only movement changes the SGR 4/24 marker in the screen
when the cursor is visible. CSI ?25l hides it; CSI ?25h shows it.

.screen emits ESC[7m/ESC[0m for inverse cells and ESC[4m/ESC[24m
around the cursor cell, even if it is a blank. Underline means terminal
cursor (not a vi file position). When hidden, no cursor marker is
emitted. Reads may split an ANSI sequence.
